  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 155 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nFriday...Although a bit of pseudo monsoonal moisture at mid \nlevels will be over our forecast area, the deeper moisture on \nsouthwest flow aloft will remain over Colorado and New Mexico, \nthus continued dry for us. 700 mb temperatures suggest summer \nlike heat will continue for warm weather lovers.\n\nSaturday...Low and mid level moisture increases somewhat via \nprecipitable water content, and the deeper moisture will create \nwidely scattered showers and a few thunderstorms from the \nLaramie Valley to near Douglas and nearby locations in the \nafternoon and evening hours. Dry elsewhere. The decent 700 mb \ntemperatures will aid in keeping similar temperatures to Friday.\n\nSunday...Beneficial rains look quite promising as there will be\nan increase in precipitable water values via a better monsoonal\nmoisture tap. The combination of passing shortwave troughs \naloft and low level mechanical lift and orographic effects will \nhelp produce scattered afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms, most numerous over and near the mountains.\n\nMonday...With adequate low and mid level moisture availability \ncombined with upslope lift and passing shortwave troughs aloft, \nexpect scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms mainly over\nand west of Interstate 25, with the showers and thunderstorms \npropagating and developing mainly east of I-25 in the evening. \nStill some uncertainty with regard to maximum temperatures, as \nan early September cold front may pass across our counties \nbringing in a refreshing respite from the heat. With still so \nmuch uncertainty, will leave the warmer temperatures as is to \nblend in with our neighboring offices, but this day will likely \nbe cooler than currently forecasted.\n\nTuesday...An autumnal like pattern greets us synoptically for\nSeptember with a rather significant shortwave trough aloft\ntraveling eastward across Montana. Still enough atmospheric\nmoisture to produce scattered showers and thunderstorms south of\na Rawlins to Scottsbluff line. Again, temperatures may be cooler\nthan currently forecasted with later model runs and trends.",
